// The **worse half** of the same gap, and the typo that actually happens.
//
// A bare unknown name fell off the end of the chain. One *with arguments* reached the branch that
// reads a derive target list, which parsed it and then threw it away -- `if (is_derive)` guarded
// only the push, not the branch. So `#[drive(Json)]` was a struct that read a derive list,
// derived nothing, and said nothing: the misspelling of the most-used attribute in the language,
// silently doing the opposite of what it says.
//
// The branch is gated on `is_derive` now, so both halves reach the same refusal.
#[drive(Json)]
struct User {
    name: string
}

fn main() {
    print(1)
}
